{"file":{"content":"The tests do not merely suggest “try Schur”; they identify why Schur becomes available precisely when the previous trace arguments fail:\n\n[\nm\\ge c\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\na\\in a_0+3^{c-m}\\mathbb Z\n\\text{ is no longer an incomplete coset constraint.}\n]\n\nSo the next proof target is not another global spectral experiment. It is a row-sum theorem for the complete word-pair kernel of\n\n[\n(T^m)^*T^m.\n]\n\n## 1. First critique: the conjecture should be stated with normalized constants\n\nIn the manuscript, the live conjecture says roughly:\n\n[\n|T_{\\chi,c,s}^{,m}|\\le (1-\\delta)^m.\n]\n\nThat is good, but I would make the normalization completely explicit. Let\n\n[\nT_{\\chi,c,s}=W_s^{-1}M_{\\chi,c,s},\n]\n\nso the untwisted positive operator has mass (1). Then the conjecture should be:\n\n[\n\\boxed{\n\t\\exists \\alpha>1,\\ \\delta>0,\\ c_1\n\t\\quad\n\t\\forall c\\ge c_1,\\quad\n\tm=\\lfloor \\alpha c\\rfloor,\\quad\n\t|T_{\\chi,c,s}^{m}|_{2\\to2}\\le (1-\\delta)^m.\n}\n]\n\nor equivalently\n\n[\n|T_{\\chi,c,s}^{m}|_{2\\to2}^{1/m}\\le 1-\\delta.\n]\n\nYour table suggests at (s=0)\n\n[\n1-\\delta\\approx 0.70\n]\n\nfor (m=20), but I would not bake (0.70) into the theorem. Use it as empirical scale.\n\nAlso specify whether (s) is fixed or uniform over (s\\in[0,1]). The proof may be easiest first at (s=0). I would state the first theorem for (s=0), then add a uniform-(s) extension as a later target.\n\n## 2. What the Schur proof must actually show\n\nLet\n\n[\nK_m(x,y)\n]\n\nbe the kernel of (T^m). Then\n\n[\n(T^m)^*T^m\n]\n\nhas kernel\n\n[\nH_m(y,y')\n=========\n\n\\sum_x \\overline{K_m(x,y)}K_m(x,y').\n]\n\nThe Schur test wants a bound like\n\n[\n\\sup_y\\sum_{y'} |H_m(y,y')|\n\\le (1-\\delta)^{2m}.\n]\n\nBecause\n\n[\n|T^m|^2\n=======\n\n|(T^m)^*T^m|\n\\le\n\\sup_y\\sum_{y'} |H_m(y,y')|.\n]\n\nThe proof has to avoid the trace ceiling by using **row sums**, not trace sums:\n\n[\n\\operatorname{tr}H_m\n]\n\ncounts too many small modes. Row-sum control asks whether a fixed input state can coherently feed many deep-stationary outputs. Your eigenvector tests suggest no.\n\nSo the next section should be titled something like:\n\n[\n\\textbf{The complete-kernel Schur problem.}\n]\n\n## 3. Write the entries of (H_m) explicitly\n\nFor a word (w=(b_0,\\dots,b_{m-1})), keep the affine formula\n\n[\nS_w(a)\n======\n\n(3^m a+C_w)2^{-B_m(w)}.\n]\n\nThe (m)-step kernel should be a sum over words (w) that take a source residue (a) to target residue (x):\n\n[\nx=S_w(a).\n]\n\nThus an entry of (H_m) between (a) and (a') should have the form\n\n[\nH_m(a,a')\n=========\n\n\\sum_{w,w'}\np_s(w)p_s(w')\n\\sum_{x:,x=S_w(a)=S_{w'}(a')}\n\\chi!\\left(\\Phi_w(a)\\Phi_{w'}(a')^{-1}\\right).\n]\n\nEquivalently, after solving the common-endpoint constraint, this becomes a word-pair sum over ((w,w')) and source variables satisfying\n\n[\nS_w(a)=S_{w'}(a').\n]\n\nFor (m\\ge c), the source constraint is complete, so the inner sum should be over all of (U_{3^c}) or a complete union of units. That is where the exact vanishing lemmas apply entrywise.\n\nThe first proof task is simply to put this formula in a clean proposition:\n\n[\n\\textbf{Complete-kernel formula for }H_m.\n]\n\nDo not try to estimate yet. First make the kernel exact.\n\n## 4. Split the row sum into four classes\n\nFor each fixed row (a), split the word-pair contribution into:\n\n[\n\\text{I. nonstationary pairs;}\n]\n\n[\n\\text{II. thin pairs;}\n]\n\n[\n\\text{III. true-stationary prefix-locked pairs;}\n]\n\n[\n\\text{IV. transition/degenerate-minimum pairs.}\n]\n\nYou already have tools for I–III.\n\n### I. Nonstationary pairs\n\nExact coset vanishing should kill them entrywise when (m\\ge c):\n\n[\n\\sum_{\\text{complete source coset}}\\chi(R_{w,w'}(u))=0.\n]\n\nSo class I contributes zero.\n\n### II. Thin pairs\n\nUse the thin-sector bound you proved. Since it is already\n\n[\n\\Theta_{m,c}\\to0\n]\n\nfor (m\\le 0.7c), check the exact range. Your new Schur regime is (m\\ge c), so the old thin bound may not apply directly. You need either:\n\n1. a new thin-pair estimate for (m=\\alpha c), (\\alpha\\ge1), or\n2. show thin pairs are included in exact vanishing once endpoints are complete.\n\nThis is a point to check carefully. Do not assume the old (m\\le0.7c) lemma covers (m\\ge c).\n\n### III. True-stationary prefix-locked pairs\n\nThese are the dangerous class. But in row-sum form they should no longer be marginal if the stationary modes are ejected.\n\nFor true-stationary pairs,\n\n[\nC_w=C_{w'}\n]\n\nor the appropriate prefix-locking condition. Their pair mass gives the dichotomy constant\n\n[\n\\Delta_s^{m-1}\n]\n\nat trace level. In row-sum form, you need to show they do not connect coherently across many columns.\n\nThis is likely where the (\\chi(2^{\\Delta B})) geometric damping lemma enters. The stationary pair differs essentially only in the last branch or in a low-dimensional suffix, giving phase\n\n[\n\\chi(2^{B_m(w')-B_m(w)}).\n]\n\nSumming over last branches gives the factor\n\n[\n\\left|\n\\sum_b p_s(b)\\chi(2)^{-b}\n\\right|.\n]\n\nFor primitive high conductor, this factor should be (<1), and for the coboundary cases it is exactly the classified exception. Make this precise.\n\n### IV. Degenerate-minimum/transition pairs\n\nYour coset dichotomy says the boundary (d=m-1) issue is gone after true pointwise valuation. Degenerate minima migrate into (d\\ge m), but each costs a mod-(3) condition. In row-sum form, these should be a geometrically thin exceptional class.\n\nYou need a clean lemma:\n\n[\n\\textbf{Degenerate-minimum mass lemma.}\n]\n\nThe total row mass of pairs whose nominal nonstationarity degenerates to (d\\ge m) is\n\n[\n\\le C\\rho^m\n]\n\nfor some (\\rho<1), or at least (o((1-\\delta)^{2m})).\n\nIf this lemma is false, the Schur estimate may fail. So test it explicitly.\n\n## 5. The likely Schur bound shape\n\nThe hoped row-sum bound probably has the form\n\n[\n\\sum_{a'}|H_m(a,a')|\n\\le\n\\Delta_s^m\n+\nE_{\\mathrm{thin}}(m,c)\n+\nE_{\\mathrm{deg}}(m,c)\n+\nE_{\\mathrm{tail}}(B,m).\n]\n\nAt (s=0),\n\n[\n\\Delta_0^m=3^{-m}.\n]\n\nBut (|T^m|^2) is not of size (3^{-m}) in your table. If\n\n[\n|T^{20}|^{1/20}\\approx0.70,\n]\n\nthen\n\n[\n|T^{20}|^2\\approx0.70^{40}\\approx 6.4\\times10^{-7}.\n]\n\nThis is close to\n\n[\n3^{-20}\\approx2.9\\times10^{-10}\n]\n\nonly up to large dimensional factors. So the row sum is not simply (\\Delta^m); it includes endpoint multiplicity.\n\nA more realistic bound is:\n\n[\n\\sum_{a'}|H_m(a,a')|\n\\le\nC_m \\Delta_s^m\n]\n\nwith\n\n[\nC_m^{1/(2m)}\n]\n\nbounded mildly, giving the observed (0.70). The previous trace ceiling had a dimension factor; Schur should replace it by a much smaller row-multiplicity factor.\n\nSo when you compute row sums, record the effective constant:\n\n[\nA_m(c)=\\sup_a\\sum_{a'}|H_m(a,a')|/\\Delta_s^m.\n]\n\nThen\n\n[\n|T^m|^{1/m}\\le A_m(c)^{1/(2m)}\\sqrt{\\Delta_s}.\n]\n\nYour observed (0.70) versus (\\sqrt{\\Delta_0}=0.577) suggests\n\n[\nA_m^{1/(2m)}\\approx 1.21.\n]\n\nFor (m=20), that means (A_m\\approx 1.21^{40}\\approx 2000). Not tiny, but subexponential enough. The proof only needs\n\n[\nA_m(c)\\le e^{o(m)}\n]\n\nor\n\n[\nA_m(c)\\le C^m\n]\n\nwith\n\n[\nC^{1/2}\\sqrt{\\Delta_s}<1.\n]\n\nAt (s=0), this means\n\n[\nC<3.\n]\n\nThis is an important numerical threshold. The Schur bookkeeping must beat a row-multiplicity growth rate of (3^m). It likely does.\n\n## 6. Immediate numerical row-sum audit\n\nBefore proving, compute:\n\n[\nR_m(c)=\\sup_a\\sum_{a'}|H_m(a,a')|.\n]\n\nThen report:\n\n[\nR_m(c)^{1/(2m)}\n]\n\nand compare to\n\n[\n\\sigma_1(T^m)^{1/m}.\n]\n\nIf Schur is sharp, they should be close. If Schur is much larger, you need a weighted Schur test or a better norm.\n\nAlso compute the decomposition:\n\n[\nR_m=R_{\\mathrm{stat}}+R_{\\mathrm{thin}}+R_{\\mathrm{deg}}+R_{\\mathrm{tail}}.\n]\n\nThis will tell exactly which class controls the bound.\n\nUse (m=c,c+2,2c) for (c=5,6,7), not only fixed (m=20).\n\n## 7. Try a weighted Schur test\n\nThe unweighted row sum may not be optimal. Since the operator is not normal, use the Perron weights of the untwisted positive operator or the approximate singular vector weights.\n\nA weighted Schur test says:\n\n[\n|H|\\le\n\\left(\n\\sup_a \\frac{1}{r(a)}\\sum_{a'} |H(a,a')|r(a')\n\\right)\n]\n\nfor any positive (r).\n\nTry:\n\n[\nr(a)=\\text{right Perron vector of }|T|^m\n]\n\nor, if the positive operator is uniform, (r=1).\n\nNumerically compare:\n\n[\n\\text{unweighted Schur},\\quad\n\\text{Perron-weighted Schur},\\quad\n\\sigma_1^2.\n]\n\nIf the weighted Schur is close to (\\sigma_1^2), use it in the proof.\n\n## 8. Stationary-sector ejection should become a lemma\n\nYour measurement\n\n[\n\\rho(P_{\\rm stat}T^mP_{\\rm stat})^{1/m}\\le 3\\times10^{-4}\n]\n\nis very strong. It suggests a structural lemma:\n\n[\n\\boxed{\n\tP_{\\rm stat}T P_{\\rm stat}=0\n}\n]\n\nor at least\n\n[\nP_{\\rm stat}T P_{\\rm stat}\n]\n\ndrops conductor immediately and cannot return without a high-conductor phase.\n\nTry to prove:\n\n[\nT(\\mathcal H_{\\rm stat})\\subseteq \\mathcal H_{\\rm high},\n]\n\nwhere (\\mathcal H_{\\rm high}) is orthogonal to (\\mathcal H_{\\rm stat}), and\n\n[\nP_{\\rm stat}T(\\mathcal H_{\\rm stat})=0.\n]\n\nIf true, the stationary trace mass is provably nilpotent. That is exactly the eigenvector-aware correction trace lacked.\n\nThis lemma may be easier than the full Schur estimate and should be attacked first.\n\n## 9. Suggested proof order\n\nProceed in this order.\n\n### Step A: Complete-kernel formula\n\nWrite (H_m=(T^m)^*T^m) entries as complete word-pair sums for (m\\ge c).\n\n### Step B: Exact annihilation classes\n\nProve which word-pair classes vanish entrywise by coset dichotomy.\n\n### Step C: Stationary-sector ejection\n\nProve (P_{\\rm stat}T^mP_{\\rm stat}) is nilpotent/small.\n\n### Step D: Row-sum audit\n\nCompute and then prove the Schur row bound.\n\n### Step E: Weighted Schur refinement\n\nIf unweighted Schur misses the observed norm, introduce weights.\n\n## 10. Manuscript framing\n\nThe new subsection is good. I would add one sentence to prevent overclaiming:\n\n> The numerical singular-value data do not yet prove the uniform gap; they identify the correct non-trace norm in which the already-proved vanishing and flattening lemmas should be assembled.\n\nThen state the Schur conjecture as the active finite-side conjecture.\n\nThe routes-status paragraph is exactly right. Keep it.\n\n## Bottom line\n\nThe finite side’s live theorem is now:\n\n[\n\\boxed{\n\t\\text{large-}m\\text{ complete-kernel Schur contraction.}\n}\n]\n\nThe next concrete deliverables are:\n\n[\n\\boxed{\n\t\\text{1. exact formula for }(T^m)^*T^m\\text{ entries when }m\\ge c;}\n]\n\n[\n\\boxed{\n\t\\text{2. row-sum decomposition by stationary/nonstationary/thin/degenerate classes;}\n\t]\n\t\n\t[\n\t\\boxed{\n\t\t\\text{3. proof that stationary trace mass is nilpotent/ejected, not spectral;}\n\t\t]\n\t\t\n\t\t[\n\t\t\\boxed{\n\t\t\t\\text{4. weighted Schur estimate giving }|T^m|^{1/m}<1.\n\t\t}\n\t\t]\n\t\t\n\t\tIf that works, the high-conductor damping conjecture closes.\n","filePath":"/home/john/mynotes/deterministic_equidistribution_theory/critiques/dc_19.md","numLines":534,"startLine":1,"totalLines":534},"type":"text"}